<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra Aphai Mani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phra Aphai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> is an epic verse romance by the Thai poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a>, composed in instalments over some two decades in the early nineteenth century. It tells of a prince who prefers the magic flute to swordsmanship and his adventures with giants, mermaids and Western sea-farers. One of the longest poems in Thai, it is a classic of Thai literature, still taught in schools.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-aphai-mani.jpg" alt="Phra Aphai Mani" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunrise_on_Hat_Saikaew,_Koh_Samet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
